---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Kilnbook for schools and colleges"
	description="Booking and firing software for school and college ceramics departments: wheel time between classes, a firing queue for one or two kilns, and courses that follow the semester timetable."
>
	<section class="band-dark page-hero">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">For schools &amp; colleges</p>
			<h1>A ceramics room that runs on the timetable.</h1>
			<p class="lede narrow">
				One or two kilns, a room full of wheels and a few hundred students who want to throw between
				classes. Kilnbook keeps track of who is on which wheel, what is in each load and which
				section meets on Tuesday afternoons.
			</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Wheel time between classes</h2>
		<p>
			Students reserve a wheel or a hand-building table in one-hour blocks from their phone, so a free
			period turns into studio time instead of a queue at the door. You set how many hours each
			student gets a week and how far ahead they can book. A tablet by the door shows who is in the
			room while the kilns are running.
			<a href="/features/wheel-time">How wheel booking works</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>One queue per kiln</h2>
		<p>
			Students log a piece when they set it on the bisque shelf, and it goes into the right load for
			your kiln. Technicians and teachers see how full each bisque and glaze load is, mark it as fired
			with one tap, and keep a firing log with the date, cone, schedule and who loaded it. When a load
			comes out, students get a text that their work is ready to collect.
			<a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Classes that follow the semester</h2>
		<p>
			Set each section up once as a course that runs for the semester, like Ceramics I on Mondays and
			Wednesdays. Instructors get their own login with the roster, mark attendance from the studio
			tablet and leave notes for whoever teaches the next section. Evening or community classes can
			sit alongside them, with waitlists that fill cancelled seats.
			<a href="/features/classes">How classes work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Which plan fits a school?</h2>
		<p>
			The Starter plan covers one kiln. The Studio plan covers up to 3 kilns, with unlimited members
			and wheel and table reservations, which suits most departments with one or two kilns. Collective
			has no kiln limit and covers more than one location. School ceramics programs get 30% off any
			plan. <a href="/pricing">Compare plans</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Questions departments ask</h2>
		<div class="faq">
			<details>
				<summary>We only have one kiln. Is Kilnbook still worth it?</summary>
				<p>
					Yes. The Starter plan covers one kiln, and the firing queue is most useful when a single
					kiln is shared by every class. If you add a second kiln later, the Studio plan covers up
					to 3.
				</p>
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>How do students know when their work is out of the kiln?</summary>
				<p>
					When a load is unloaded, every student with work in it gets a text with a photo of the
					shelf. No more "is my bowl out yet?" at the start of every class.
				</p>
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>Is there a discount for schools?</summary>
				<p>
					School ceramics programs and registered nonprofits get 30% off any plan. Mention it when
					you book a demo.
				</p>
			</details>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Get your ceramics room off the clipboard.</h2>
			<p>We will show you Kilnbook set up for your kilns, your wheels and your timetable.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
